        "ratio_two_sd_to_band": 1.0,
        "band_resolvable_by_one_draw": true,
        "what_is_held_fixed": "allocation prefix and allocation ordinal, hence relative placement; the process, its address-space layout and the clock state are not"
      },
      "why_the_round_range_is_not_this": "the seven-round range (median 0.612% at this size) describes the sample the estimator minimises over, not the estimator. It is reported in `within_draw_instrument_floor` and is NOT used for the resolvability flag above.",
      "conclusion": "the 20 slot means span 18.20% and the band is 0.2047% wide. Their spacing is clustered rather than even: 8 of 19 adjacent gaps are narrower than the band and the largest is 20.0x it. Zero draws in band is therefore not evidence against the historical value -- with means bunched this way, a narrow window can sit in a sparse stretch and be missed by every draw. No probability is claimed for that; an earlier version asserted one from a uniform model that this same spacing refutes. The defensible statement is symmetric: at this size and sample size the data neither authenticate nor exclude a value in the band. On whether one draw could resolve the band at all: repeating the published min-of-seven estimator at a fixed (prefix, ordinal) cell across 4 processes gives a median RSD of 0.098%, so 2sd is 0.197% against a 0.2047% band -- a ratio of 1.0. The estimator IS repeatable to about the band width at this size, so resolution is not the obstacle here; the clustered spacing above is. An earlier version claimed the opposite from the seven-round range, which is a property of the sample the min is taken over, not of the min."
